                <h2>Why Your Budget Always Runs Out</h2>
<p>Many landing page operators face this situation: the ad account has plenty of funds, but after two weeks, they're already running short. Popular channels haven't been fully tested, yet they're afraid to try new ones. The problem usually isn't the bid on a single ad, but the lack of overall budget planning.</p>
<p>This article doesn't discuss macro market trends. Instead, it gives you a budget allocation checklist you can implement immediately.</p>
<h2>Step 1: Complete These 5 Checks Before Allocating</h2>
<p>Before distributing money to various channels, first confirm whether your baseline data is in place. Many people skip this step, causing budget allocation to become "gut feeling."</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] <strong>Define your core metrics</strong>: Are you pursuing ROAS, CAC, or new customer conversions? Different goals correspond to different budget allocation logic.</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Calculate your monthly spending capacity</strong>: Work backward from your ad account's monthly budget to determine weekly and daily spendable amounts. A tool app team's experience: reserve 30% of the monthly budget as discretionary funds.</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Confirm conversion attribution windows</strong>: Different platforms have different attribution periods (Google 28 days, Meta 7 days), which directly affects the evaluation cycle for each channel's budget.</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Check your creative inventory</strong>: Budget allocation must match creative quantity. Each channel needs at least 3-5 sets of differentiated creatives before scaling.</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Assess your team's response speed</strong>: If you only have one person managing accounts, don't open too many channels simultaneously—otherwise, optimization can't keep up and budgets are wasted.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Step 2: Allocate Budget by Channel Characteristics</h2>
<p>There's no standard answer for budget ratios, but there are reasonable reference ranges. Based on common landing page scenarios, the recommended allocation logic is:</p>
<h3>Testing Phase (0-4 weeks): Explore Channels and Creatives</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Channel</th>
<th>Recommended Ratio</th>
<th>Core Objective</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Meta (Facebook/Instagram)</td>
<td>40-50%</td>
<td>Rapid creative testing, data accumulation</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Google Search</td>
<td>20-30%</td>
<td>Test high-intent keywords</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>TikTok</td>
<td>15-20%</td>
<td>Short video creative testing</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Other channels (Pinterest)</td>
<td>5-10%</td>
<td>Supplementary traffic testing</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Note</strong>: Each channel needs to run for at least 500 conversions before judging performance—otherwise, data samples are insufficient and easy to misjudge.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Stabilization Phase (after 4 weeks): Dynamic Adjustment Based on Performance</h3>
<p>On a weekly cycle, reallocate based on each channel's ROAS and CAC performance:</p>
<ul>
<li>ROAS &gt; 3 and CAC acceptable: Gradually increase budget</li>
<li>ROAS between 1.5-3: Maintain status quo or small-scale testing</li>
<li>ROAS &lt; 1.5 or CAC exceeds tolerance: Pause or reduce budget</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Practical operation</strong>: Suppose your monthly budget is $100,000. After the testing phase, if one Google channel achieves ROAS of 4, shift that budget from underperforming channels.</p>
<h2>Step 3: Set Budget Alerts and Reconciliation Rhythm</h2>
<p>Budget allocation isn't just about "how much money to distribute," but also "when to stop and when to refill."</p>
<h3>Daily/Weekly Checklist</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Daily check: Is each channel's spend approaching its budget cap (need attention if over 80%)?</li>
<li>[ ] Daily check: Are abnormal spends from creative testing (avoid invalid creatives continuing to drain budget)?</li>
<li>[ ] Weekly reconciliation: Actual spend vs planned allocation—deviation over 15% requires cause analysis</li>
<li>[ ] Weekly review: Cost-per-conversion trends, identifying which ad sets are raising overall CAC</li>
</ul>
<h3>Monthly Financial Reconciliation Checklist</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Do top-up records match account spending (prevent platform billing errors)?</li>
<li>[ ] Have refunds/chargebacks been processed promptly?</li>
<li>[ ] Cash flow management under prepaid vs. postpaid models</li>
<li>[ ] Budget balance alerts set (most platforms support automatic reminders)</li>
</ul>
<h2>Step 4: Avoid These 3 Common Mistakes</h2>
<ol>
<li>
<p><strong>Putting all eggs in one basket</strong>: Only investing in Meta or Google. One cross-border team had their Meta account banned, completely disrupting that month's sales plan. Recommendation: maintain at least 2 main channels.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Over-dispersing testing phase budget</strong>: Investing $100/day in each channel, resulting in no channel generating effective data. Recommendation: focus on 1-2 main channels for concentrated testing.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Ignoring the match between budget and creatives</strong>: Allocating budget without prepared creatives, leading to idle spending. Before launching: confirm whether this creative set has been validated on this channel?</p>
</li>
</ol>
<h2>Quick Reference: Budget Allocation Self-Check Table</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Checklist Item</th>
<th>Pass Standard</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Is monthly budget broken down to daily level?</td>
<td>Executable daily</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Is 20-30% contingency reserved?</td>
<td>Has emergency funds</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Does channel count match team capability?</td>
<td>No more than 3 main channels</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Are daily spend caps set?</td>
<td>Prevents overspending</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Is there a weekly review mechanism?</td>
<td>At least once weekly</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Is creative inventory sufficient?</td>
<td>3-5 sets per channel</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Is top-up method stable?</td>
<td>Avoids account balance shortages</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h2>Closing</h2>
<p>Budget allocation isn't set in stone—it continuously adjusts as test data accumulates. The core principle is: <strong>test with small budgets, scale with large budgets</strong>. Start with a reasonable ratio, run the numbers, then tilt based on actual performance.</p>
<p>If you're struggling with "how to manage multi-channel budgets," start with the 5 checklist items from Step 1. Get the baseline data clear first, then discuss allocation strategy.</p>
